    "guide": {
      "schemaVersion": 1,
      "installation": "Install with your plugin manager. For lazy.nvim:\n\n```lua\n{\n  \"living-code-theme/nvim\",\n  priority = 1000,\n  config = function()\n    require(\"living-code\").setup({\n      flavor = \"night\",\n      transparent = false,\n      italic_comments = true,\n    })\n    vim.cmd.colorscheme(\"living-code\")\n  end,\n}\n```\n\nOr pick a flavor directly: `:colorscheme living-code-signal`. Covers Tree-sitter, LSP semantic tokens, diagnostics, gitsigns, telescope, nvim-cmp / blink.cmp, which-key, mini.*, lualine (`theme = \"living-code\"`).",
      "configuration": "Call setup before loading the colorscheme. All options are optional:\n\n```lua\nrequire(\"living-code\").setup({\n  flavor = \"night\",\n  light_flavor = \"paper\",\n  transparent = false,\n  italic_comments = true,\n  dim_inactive = false,\n  on_highlights = nil,\n})\nvim.cmd.colorscheme(\"living-code\")\n```\n\nWith `background=light`, the default colorscheme selects `light_flavor`. A flavor-specific colorscheme always loads that flavor. For lualine, use `theme = \"living-code\"`.",
      "compatibility": "Uses Neovim's Lua highlight API and true color. Includes Tree-sitter, LSP semantic tokens, diagnostics, gitsigns, telescope, nvim-cmp / blink.cmp, which-key, mini.* and lualine highlights. Plugin integrations are optional; install the plugins you use separately.",
